WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:09.880
Hydrogen burns in oxygen to make water. Write it down and count the atoms on each side. Something does not add up. Can you spot it?

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:14.360
Count the oxygen atoms on each side. Which side is short?

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:27.680
Atoms are never created or destroyed in a reaction. So each element must have the same count on both sides.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:42.520
Left side: two hydrogen and two oxygen. Right side: two hydrogen and one oxygen. Oxygen is short on the right. Put a two in front of hydrogen and a two in front of water.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:54.600
A tempting fix is to change a subscript, so water becomes hydrogen peroxide. The counts can match, but that is a different compound. Only coefficients may change.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:07.240
Balance by changing coefficients, never subscripts. Two hydrogen molecules and one oxygen molecule give two water molecules: four hydrogen and two oxygen on each side.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:11.720
